# Statistical Analysis

This folder contains statistical testing and modeling used to evaluate relationships between critical violations and inspection outcomes.

## Purpose

The goal of this analysis is to determine whether critical violations are associated with failed inspections and increased odds of facility closure.

## Methods Used

### 1. Chi-Square Test

A chi-square test was used to evaluate whether inspection outcomes were associated with the presence of critical violations.

#### Null Hypothesis (H0)
There is no relationship between inspection outcome and the presence of critical violations.

#### Alternative Hypothesis (H1)
There is a relationship between inspection outcome and the presence of critical violations.

The chi-square test compares passed and failed inspections against whether at least one critical violation was present.

---

### 2. Logistic Regression

A logistic regression model was used to estimate how increases in critical violations affect the odds of facility closure.

#### Model
- Outcome variable: `is_closed`
- Predictor variable: `critical_count`

The model estimates whether inspections with more critical violations are more likely to result in closure outcomes.

---

## Main Findings

- Failed inspections were more likely to contain critical violations than passed inspections.
- Higher critical violation counts were associated with increased odds of closure outcomes.
- Most passed inspections had zero critical violations, while failed inspections showed a wider distribution of critical findings.
